  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity for effective social dialogue and labour market governance</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social dialogue in Eswatini remains fragile, with no clear criteria to identify the most representative workers and employers&#x2019; organizations. The ILO provides capacity support to help establish a more effective social dialogue model, addressing representation gaps and strengthening tripartism. Public service bargaining faces challenges in implementing collective agreements and addressing pay disparities. The ILO supports trust-building between workers and government to sustain an effective bargaining framework. Labour dispute resolution is constrained by long delays and legislative gaps. In collaboration with government and social partners, the ILO supports the review of standard operating procedures of the Commission for Mediation, Arbitration and Conciliation (CMAC) to improve efficiency and access. Technical assistance also contributes to reviewing the Labour Relations Amendment Bill to strengthen CMAC effectiveness. The labour inspectorate has limited capacity to enforce labour laws due to resource constraints. The ILO works in partnership with the government and a range of public agencies across sectors to develop Memorandums of Understanding to strengthen coordinated labour law enforcement. Training on ethical conduct is also provided to improve inspectors&#x2019; efficiency and accountability. The Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) Act is outdated and enforcement remains weak. Working with the Technical Committee on Occupational Health and Safety (TCOSH), the ILO provides supports the review of the Act in line with international standards and emerging risks. While labour statistics have improved through an updated Labour Force Survey methodology, gaps remain in comparability and in establishing an integrated Labour Market Information System. The ILO continues to strengthen statistical systems and capacity. Beneficiaries comprise institutions supporting social dialogue, labour governance, labour dispute management, labour data systems, and the workers.</narrative>
  </description>
